cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its benchmark results in your research, please cite it as below."
title: "mode-resolved-calibration: mode-resolved calibration-confidence continuum for photometric inverse problems"
type: software
authors:
  - alias: dddsx3
    name: "GMX"
    email: "3570239187@qq.com"
version: 0.3.0
date-released: 2026-09-10
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/dddsx3/mode-resolved-calibration"
abstract: >-
  A Python library for mode-resolved calibration-confidence analysis of
  linearized inverse problems with structured nuisance. This release also serves as the
  reproducibility package for the associated research manuscript (forthcoming):
  Schur-complement Delta-Fisher information, calibration-retention spectra, the
  gauge closed-form spectral response, and the mode-resolved severity analysis on
  OpenIllumination (11 objects x 6 levels, 20 seeds; paper-facing corrected
  interface: directional validation of the retention ordering, within-cell
  Spearman RA = 0.90, CI [0.70, 0.95] — rank-equivalent to the mode-index
  baseline by construction — plus a quantified real-data amplitude validity
  envelope for the linearized theory). Includes a preregistered calibration-allocation
  evaluation on the 11 held-out objects (29,700 reconstructions): mode-derived
  guidance improves reconstruction over random allocation (an actionable
  outcome vs random), classical E/A/D-optimal greedy baselines achieve modestly
  lower AUC in post-hoc paired comparisons, and an active-set random control
  shows the benefit over full-universe random is an active-set effect rather
  than a mode-ordering effect. v0.3.0 closes the math-freeze correctness gates
  (noise-fit coefficient order, normalized dual-coordinate mode projection,
  singular-covariance routes, retention-covariance theorem, allocation rank
  invariance) with a preregistered A/B/C/D factorial rerun: the within-cell
  directional-validation result is unchanged under the corrected interface
  (arm D is the paper-facing record). The active research direction is a
  certified analysis of calibration-precision allocation (exact convex
  reformulation, low-rank retention structure, Frank-Wolfe certificates).
  Ships frozen results, independent headline recomputation tests (pytest),
  and figure/table regeneration scripts.
keywords:
  - photometric inverse problems
  - calibration
  - Fisher information
  - Schur complement
  - spectral analysis
  - OpenIllumination